---
import Pipeline from "./Pipeline.astro";
import AskKick from "./AskKick.astro";
import PartHead from "./PartHead.astro";
import NextPart from "./NextPart.astro";
import Beat from "./Beat.astro";
import Touchline from "./Touchline.astro";
import { run, scheduleLog } from "../../lib/first-run";
import { story } from "../../lib/coverage";
import { pipeline, spell, toPlay } from "../../lib/cases";

const sources = [
  ["pages", "Count the matches listed on the pages Kick saved"],
  ["log", "Count the lines in Kick's fetch log"],
  ["rule", "The membership list and the competition's format"],
];
const bar = (n: number) => `${(n / story.scheduled) * 100}%`;
const chain = [
  [story.scheduled, "scheduled, by the rule", ""],
  [story.finals, "finished", `${toPlay} still to be played`],
  [story.afterRepair, "with a score on a saved page", "1 final with no score"],
  [story.counted, "read into Kick's table", `${story.afterRepair - story.counted} on the page that never arrived`],
] as const;
---

<section id="case-dunmore">
  <PartHead id="case-dunmore">Case 1. Dunmore: a count needs an expected set</PartHead>
  <Pipeline small lit="scrape" note="This case happens at Scrape" />

  <p class="cv-callout">Dunmore athletics: “You've got our men at six conference games. We've played thirteen.” Kick's table had no way to know thirteen was the right number.</p>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Look" />
  <p>Besides the table, Kick kept a log of the {run.total} pages it requested. {pipeline.boxes} of those were box scores from Alder Ridge. Here are the {spell(pipeline.asked)} schedule pages:</p>
  <table class="cv-log">
    <caption>Kick's fetch log, schedule pages</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">School</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{scheduleLog.map((l) => <tr class:list={{ "is-gap": l.result !== "saved" }}><th scope="row">{l.name}</th><td>{l.result}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The failure sits right there in one line of the log, yet Kick's summary never mentions it. Try asking Kick a follow-up question:</p>
  <AskKick />

  <h4 class="cv-try">Build the denominator</h4>
  <p>Kick's log names the missing page, but it still can't tell you how many matches are missing, because nothing in the run records how many matches should exist. The conference format can.</p>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="scheduled">
    <p class="cv-kind">Knowledge check</p>
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="rule" data-why="The membership list and the competition format. Both exist before the run and independently of it, so a silent school stays in the count." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ pages: "A school whose page never arrived lists nothing, so it drops out of its own count.", log: "The log only counts pages requested, not matches." })}>
      <label><span>Where can the expected number of matches come from, before the run?</span>
        <select data-save="q-source"><option value="">Choose</option>{sources.map(([k, t]) => <option value={k}>{t}</option>)}</select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer={story.scheduled} data-why={`${story.scheduled}. ${spell(story.schools, true)} hosts, each with ${spell(story.schools - 1)} visitors: ${story.schools} × ${story.schools - 1}. You don't need to fetch a single page to know that.`}>
      <label for="cv-scheduled">Each of the {spell(story.schools)} schools hosts every other school once. How many conference matches are in the full season?</label>
      <input id="cv-scheduled" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="q-scheduled" />
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Answer both, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answers</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>Of those {story.scheduled} scheduled matches, {spell(toPlay)} haven't been played yet on Friday.</p></div>
  </div>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Name it" />
  <p>Kick never wrote down an expected set, so it had no way to calculate coverage: just {story.counted} matches found, compared against nothing. Its log could name the silent page because it had a list of pages. For matches, it had no list at all.</p>
  <p>Survey statisticians call the list of units you set out to collect the <strong>sampling frame</strong>, and a unit on that list that doesn't answer <strong>unit nonresponse</strong>. Dunmore's schedule page is unit nonresponse.</p>

  <Touchline part="case-dunmore" at={["Fetch"]} note="At Fetch, before the first request.">
    <p>Touchline's expected list comes from a season membership file kept separately from any scraper run, and expected match totals come from the conference format. Counting whatever appears on collected pages is a weaker fallback, because it is only as complete as the pages that arrived.</p>
  </Touchline>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Your claim" />
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="claim-count">
    <p class="cv-kind">Apply it</p>
    <div data-q data-type="range" data-answer={`${story.counted},${story.finals}`} data-why={`${story.counted} of ${story.finals}. Kick's summary reported ${story.counted}. The denominator is what should exist by now: ${story.scheduled} scheduled, minus ${spell(toPlay)} still to play. Not ${story.scheduled} (unplayed matches aren't missing) and not ${story.counted} (a count of what arrived can't show what didn't).`}>
      <p class="cv-qlabel">Write the caption Kick's table needed. Kick's summary only stated how many matches it found.</p>
      <p class="cv-row cv-fillin">
        <span>Built from</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Matches in the table" data-save="claim-count" />
        <span>of</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Finished conference matches" data-save="claim-total" />
        <span>finished conference matches.</span>
      </p>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Enter both numbers to check and see the full breakdown.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check caption</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <div class="cv-chain" role="img" aria-label={chain.map(([n, what, lost]) => `${n} ${what}${lost ? `, minus ${lost}` : ""}`).join(". ")}>
        {chain.map(([n, what, lost]) => <div>{lost && <p class="cv-drop">minus {lost}</p>}<span class="cv-track"><span style={`width:${bar(n)}`}></span></span><p><b>{n}</b> {what}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <p>Riley could have seen every line of this before publishing. With a denominator, any reader can tell the table is short. Next, let's see which rows are affected.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="cv-further">Optional deep dive: <a href="../01-collection-field-work/?from=case-dunmore#statistics">Statistics of the gap</a> covers sampling frames and survey terminology in more detail.</p>
  <NextPart from="case-dunmore">Next, how one missing page reached every team in the table.</NextPart>
</section>
